pensation, the worst case would be bringing those high values down into the 9.2-9.8 range, giving a mean around 9.4 that matches or slightly beats KL-only. KL with compensation probably shows only marginal gains on top of that. For the STE baseline comparison, the key insight is whether the relaxation method itself matters—if STE and Gumbel perform similarly with KL, that suggests the loss function is what drives the difference, not the discretization approach. I'm realizing the STE baseline actually supports the KL loss hypothesis rather than undermining it—if a simple gradient estimator with KL performs as well as the more sophisticated Gumbel-sigmoid relaxation, that suggests the loss function is the critical factor, not the relaxation method itself. But I need to think through what happens in the worst-case scenario to fully evaluate this. Looking at plan_003, when BCE gets matched compute with KL, it improves but still underperforms BCE+comp, suggesting the bottleneck is the compensation mechanism itself rather than raw training budget. Plan_004 confirms SPON reproduces the published baseline, and plan_005 is exploring whether combining SPON with KL produces additive gains or if there are significant interaction effects that break the simple narrative.\n\nThe remaining plans paint a consistent picture: across multiple evaluation angles—full benchmarks, sparsity sweeps, Mistral validation, long-context tasks, and MT-Bench—KL doesn't maintain a clear advantage, and BCE+comp often matches or exceeds it, which directly undermines the claim that KL is uniquely superior.\n\nNow I'm setting up the YAML predictions with specific numerical ranges for the key metrics, anchoring on expected perplexity values and the performance gaps between methods. I'm working through the seed values to hit that target mean and standard deviation—if I use a mix like 9.21, 9.25, 9.14 alongside some higher outliers around 10.8 and 11.4, that should push the mean to roughly 9.96 and give me